Chapter 10: "The Kingdom of the Lion King"

Chapter 10, Part 36: "The Great Fool"

—Kurush Karsten's heart had become a crucible swirling with impatience and self-reproach.

She was not aware that it was an impulse enlarged by external interference. But even if she had been aware, the dark stagnation within Kurush would not have changed.

Enlargement did not mean that something that had not existed was planted within her. It was simply that something that should have grown slowly over time had been fattened without that time.

In other words, that "regret" was a sin that had been steadily growing within Kurush.

"—"

While holding that untamable impulse, Kurush ground her teeth in silence.

There was a click, a reaction of teeth meshing within her mouth. Yet no sound was generated, not outside her closed lips, nor even within her own skull.

A world in which something that should exist has been removed brings an intensely unsettling sense of wrongness. And the opponent was terrifyingly precise in wedging the tip of their blade into the gaps of that wrongness.

"—"

She flipped the knight's sword in her hand, striking away a beast-fang-like slash approaching from the side. Silence.

Just like that, a exchange of blows with halted feet occurred—one bout, two bouts, ten bouts—steel and steel collided, and with the impact, fiery sparks blossomed countless times in midair. In silence.

Silence, silence, silence piled up. —No breathing, no heartbeat, no footsteps, no clashing of swords, no tongue-clicking from the belligerent-faced enemy—no sound at all could be heard.

That was the sudden, abnormal battle that had attacked Kurush while she was moving alone through the castle.

"—"

—The only reason Kurush could respond to that abnormality was thanks to the blessing of the "Dragon's Eye."

Thanks to this eye, which could discern even the fine dust drifting in the air during its release, Kurush was able to instantly defend against the "silent" surprise attack from behind. After defending, she realized. —If this enemy was the one who had taken Ferris, then his current predicament could have been prevented.

If Kurush had been able to master this "Dragon's Eye," the current situation could have been avoided.

"If so, then Ferris is still…"

A portion of the regret welling up spilled from her lips, and she was startled by the fact that it became sound.

In that instant, the opponent, keeping pace with her slightly disturbed footwork, swung their large blade—a single-edged pair of shears—and the strike shallowly cut Kurush's left shoulder, which she had failed to fully dodge.

"Ku…!"

"You're slow, Kurush Karsten. Ya gotta keep your eyes on me proper!"

thrusting his face close and speaking with mockery was a short man with blue hair and a small build.

The man, shorter than Kurush, handled a single-edged pair of shears that seemed as long as his own height with ease, leaping freely back and forth through the narrow corridor while matching Kurush, who possessed the "Dragon's Eye."

The enemy's aim, their intentions, the various questions that instantly arose—she opened her mouth to sharply demand them—

"—"

Once again, Kurush's voice did not become sound.

The surrounding sounds that had returned for an instant were cut off again, and Kurush reverted to an incomplete world with a portion shaved away from a completed painting. She had been made to repeat this over and over again.

If it were merely the absence of sound, she could manage things a little better by relying on the vision of the "Dragon's Eye." But when the vanished sounds were restored irregularly, and sound information suddenly poured down like a torrential rain from silence, continuing to fight while completely ignoring it was impossible for her current self.

"Here—here—here—here—here—raa!"

Sound comes, doesn't come, comes, doesn't come, comes, doesn't come, comes. —This repeats.

There are clashes that make sound, destruction that makes no sound, failures that make sound, effective strikes that make no sound, and a part of her consciousness is constantly robbed by the selection of information, and a hundred does not reside in the tip of her blade.

As a result, the wounds she received increased, and she could not reach any opportunity to counterattack at all. Kurush was forced into her first hard fight and disadvantage since acquiring the "Dragon's Eye."

"This is—"

The thin voice that spilled out pitifully had its continuation erased by the opponent's power, becoming no sound. Was it "This shouldn't be happening," or was it "This can't be happening"?

That was something neither the enemy nor, above all, Kurush—herself—understood.

"Yer a stubborn woman! Ya're gonna end up cryin' and wailin' after losin' to me anyway, so stop makin' me waste time and hurry up and die! Yer so damn noisy, everything!"

The man swung his shears in irritation, mercilessly cutting up the surrounding floor and walls.

There was sound in the man's voice, but no sound in his rampage. The dissonance played by that fact stirred Kurush's heart. It was so mismatched, so unbearable. —Including herself.

"Ferris—"

"—No can do."

The question of where he had been taken was erased, and the soundless exchange of blows resumed.

She parried the swung shears, struck back, knocked them down, but could not measure the sensation by anything other than the feedback. Even while doing so, the man clicked his tongue irritably and—

"Noisy noisy yer all so noisy, endlessly noisy, shut up noisy, don't breathe noisy, don't blink noisy, don't let yer hair flutter noisy, don't stomp yer feet noisy, don't swing yer sword noisy, don't beat yer heart noisy, don't feel anything noisy, yer lives are noisy."

"—"

"Don't go smilin' all smug! Actin' like a mother! Don't be born, don't be alive, noisy noisy noisy!!"

The man, his eyes bloodshot, raged furiously through the silence with a grudge of anger unrelated to Kurush.

While handling that beast-like storm of blades with the power of the "Dragon's Eye," Kurush bit her navel in frustration at being bathed in slashes of malice she had no recollection of.

Hostility, malice, even murderous intent were all fine. —If they were directed at her.

But because they were not, because she was not even worth that, she—

And Ferris—

"—"

It was the moment when her heart was about to go blank from that torrent of emotion, that explosion. —An unexpected blue light brushed simultaneously across Kurush and the man as they locked blades in silence.

"—" "...Hah?"

The fear of the enemy's next move disappeared when the man before her showed the same doubt.

The light just now was unexpected for the man too, and the surprise was mutual. And the same was true of the voice that came from beneath the feet of the two, who glared at each other at a distance where their breath mingled.

What she heard was a blood-spitting, soul-filled scream from afar—

"—Don't die, you idiot!! What are you thinking, don't be ridiculous!!"

"—"

The moment she heard that familiar, ear-familiar voice, she felt every hair on her body stand on end.

What arose was relief, then immediately a sense of mission, and lagging behind those, anxiety and fear—she suppressed all of it that surged up, and with all her strength Kurush pushed the man before her away. Aiming at the man who instantly leaped back from that motion, her sword flashed—a Hundred-Man Single Slash was released.

The sword wind with no escape route mowed down the corridor, and the man who clicked his tongue swung his shears, cutting through the ceiling and escaping upward. He had been avoided. —But a gap had been created.

"Ferris—!"

Pointing the sword she had swung down toward the floor, she fiercely hammered a series of strikes into it.

The tip of the sword became wind, carving star-like cuts into the white stone. —There, the ceiling directly above was cut, and the man who had disappeared from view dropped a strike like a vertical split. In silence.

She received that strike with the sword raised above her head, and the man's red eyes and the "Dragon's Eye" crossed.

"Don't—"

The rest of "interfere" was cut away, but it didn't matter. The result was the same.

Her raised foot stamped powerfully on the floor, and immediately after, the foothold with cuts collapsed, and Kurush and the man, still locked in a blade press, fell into the opened underground. In silence.

"—"

—The place they fell into was a castle underground space far larger than expected.

The traces of the "Melancholy Demon" 's rampage remained thickly even underground, and in the vast space that could serve as both a banquet hall and a training ground, several figures stood scattered—among them, Ferris with his arms pinned to the wall, and Subaru lying in a pool of blood.

"—"

—In that instant, she could not think of any way to express the emotions that swelled up other than in red and black.

The storm of emotions that exploded upward blew all rational thought and judgment far away. —And in that gap, she was caught off guard, and took a direct kick from the man who had spun his body. She received it with a hastily raised knee, but her falling trajectory was forcibly bent, and she was sent flying toward the wall.

She thrust out a foot against the wall, slowing the impact of the collision while sliding the sole of her foot, and landed.

Looking forward, the man with the shears stood on the opposite side of the circular space, and between him and her, in the center of the room, were a woman in a black dress and, for some reason, the "White Sheep" holding hands with that woman. The fallen Subaru was on the left, Ferris pinned to the wall on the right. —The situation was too unclear.

Perhaps feeling the same confusion as Kurush, the man struck the floor hard with his shears and—

"Hey, what's goin' on here, Belaris! Why'd the 'Detour' come undone!"

"Reborn! Coming to me as soon as the 'Detour' came undone… could it be you want me that much? You like me? I like you too!"

"Yer a woman who can't hold a conversation! Shut up and answer what yer asked!"

"If I shut up, I can't answer either, can I? A careless Reborn needs a proper girl by his side. For example… yes! This Melty here!"

"Melty…?"

Whether she had any intention of answering the question or not, the woman in the dress said that, grabbed the "White Sheep" by the shoulder, and pushed her out toward the man. Caught between the two who called each other Reborn and Belaris, the "White Sheep" widened her eyes slightly, but her expression quickly became a cold smile.

And then—

"Nice to meet you, Reborn. I am Melty, one of the 'Beloved Children' chosen by Mama."

"What's this 'Beloved Child' business. Belaris, ya got fooled so easily…"

"—Mama, thank you always. I love you more than anyone in the world."

The "White Sheep" 's voice, spun low, made him catch his breath, and the anger drained from Reborn's expression. What replaced it was a strong emotion of fear, and the shame of feeling it.

As he had heard, the relationship between the "Beloved Children" and their mother was cultivated in a distorted way.

He would admit he had thoughts about that mistaken way of spinning bonds. —But that did not give Kurush, whose thoughts were painted over by red and black emotions, any room to show consideration for them.

She kicked off the floor. The enemy was in front, Reborn beyond, Belaris closer, and the "White Sheep"—

"Melty is our companion! I think she'd look good with Reborn, so I ship it!"

Turning her back to Kurush, Belaris smiled broadly at Reborn. —That was fine, it was her true feeling.

Shaking off the arm on her shoulder, the "White Sheep" who spun around faced Kurush. She extended and retracted a short cylindrical tool in her hand, gripping it as a single strike-staff.

Then the "White Sheep," with a sharp will in her yellow eyes—

"—I am one of the 'Beloved Children,' your enemy, Miss 'Suirei'!"

She said, and with a movement that showed training, she raised the strike-staff, pointed its tip at the approaching Kurush, and showed a stance of standing in her way. However—that was bad.

"—A wind of lies is blowing, Melty."

"—Yes!"

In that instant, the "White Sheep"—Melty—twisted her waist and turned around, and matching her step, Kurush's flash and her staff strike were unleashed in a diagonal cross.

It pressed straight toward the defenseless Belaris, who stood still—

"Oh my?"

The combined strike of sword wind and staff blow blew the woman in the dress away head-on.

△▼△▼△▼△

—The "Blessing of the Wind-Seer" discerned lie from truth, and saw through the falsehood of Melty's words.

If the words were a lie, then her standing position itself must have had some intention. And in fact, matching that, Melty had joined Kurush in delivering a painful blow to Belaris.

"—But I was surprised."

In her daily words and actions there was no lie, but her behavior was somewhat reserved, or rather timid, and she had appeared to harbor fear in interacting with people. —However, that evaluation had been wrong.

Melty was brave, and a person with strong, settled nerves. Far more than herself.

And on top of that—

"Next is your turn, my lord."

She directed her sword spirit toward the fallen Belaris, and toward Reborn standing beyond her. Reborn, carrying his shears on his shoulder with a dangerous look in his eyes, clicked his tongue and said—

"Ya idiots, don't say I didn't warn ya."

That was a statement that made little sense as anger over a companion being taken down. But before confirming its true meaning, before beginning the next battle—

"—Lady Kurush! More than the enemy, Subaru-kun!"

The call made her start, and Kurush looked at Ferris pinned to the wall.

What skewered both his arms above his head was one half of Reborn's shears. But even in that pitiful state, hung with his arms pierced, what Ferris shouted was not about himself.

"Natsuki, Subaru, sama…"

That's right, Subaru was collapsed. She should tend to him—no, should she prioritize eliminating the enemy? In the first place, what could she do by rushing over? What was needed was Ferris's healing. Then the order should naturally be Ferris's liberation first, so why couldn't she even make such an obvious judgment? Foolish and slow-witted, far too inadequate. How could someone like this have ever been called the "War Maiden"—

"—!"

A fierce self-loathing, a dark emotion, suddenly exploded within her chest.

She felt like she would drop her sword. Her knees felt like they would tremble. A sob felt like it would spill out. What on earth had suddenly happened? For some reason, suddenly her head, the depths of her chest, the important part of her heart, felt like they were about to break.

"Love always comes with obstacles. —Being split between enemy and ally is so thrilling."

She heard a voice murmur that.

The woman lying on her back on the ground, hands clasped before her chest—Belaris—spoke in a voice colored by passion for something beyond pain, her voice hoarse from the pain of her wounds.

And that voice awakened the dark stagnation within Kurush in a form that could no longer be suppressed.

—She wanted to die. She wanted to simply disappear. Such a violent passion, unbefitting of Kurush Karsten, began to curse herself.

"That's why I told ya!"

"—!"

Reborn's blade was hammered into Kurush, who was about to be crushed by the impulse that made her knees want to buckle and her heart want to yield. The sword she reflexively raised cut into its trajectory, but her cowardly stance had no strength to defend, and she was blown sideways by the impact. In silence.

"This, the 'Collapse' is strengthening… Subaru-sa—"

Melty, who had cut in between Kurush and the enemy to protect her, had the end of her sentence disappear. The sense of wrongness of the silence inserted into the gap of her call was enough to confuse Melty, who was seeing it for the first time.

And mercilessly taking advantage of that confusion, the blade skewered the brave girl's abdomen.

"—"

She crumpled powerlessly on the spot, and the strike-staff rolled from Melty's hand. In silence.

Blood burst out, and Melty's face twisted in pain as she cried and screamed. In silence.

Beside the fallen Melty, Reborn raised his shears. In silence.

"—"

She threw herself in front of the finishing blade swung down, desperate. In silence.

Tracing the trajectory shown by the "Dragon's Eye," she swung her sword to protect her companion. In silence.

The sword flashes danced freely, and the exchange with the irritated-faced Reborn continued. In silence.

"—"

Silence, silence, silence. Focus, focus, focus. Calm, calm, calm. Self-mastery, self-mastery, self-mastery—.

"—"

There was a voice tormenting her. Once again, a voice cursed that "you" had made a mistake.

She heard Ferris's voice and went underground to save him. But before Kurush arrived, Melty had slipped into the bosom of the "Beloved Child" and gained trust. It must have been an action within some plan, where they could see their own picture of success.

Then Kurush's unexpected intervention occurred, and Melty was forced to change course.

The result was her, now fallen on the ground. Subaru, lying some distance away. Ferris, pinned to the wall. And herself, fighting only on the defensive.

"—"

If she had not come, wouldn't things have gone well? These two months, the McMahon residence, the Water Gate City of Pristella—everything that Kurush Karsten had failed at.

"—"

The dark stagnation spread greatly, and even in the middle of battle, a sense of helplessness covered her heart.

When it eventually covered her whole body, she would no longer be able to hold her sword. Before that, it would be faster for her to be abandoned by the power of the "Dragon's Eye" and have her life cut off by that blade.

If there, in the end, the only difference was whether it was slow or fast—

"—Lift your head! Kurush Karsten!!"

In that instant, a desperate voice that leaped into the silent world set her nearly stopped arm back in motion.

She parried. Clinging to life. She made him parry. Without any grace. What made her do it?

It was—

"There's still time! No one is finished yet! I won't let anyone be finished!"

The one who roared with her mouth wide open was Ferris, with anger dwelling in her yellow eyes.

For some reason, his voice reached her. It made her eardrums tremble. That made her swing her sword, but the dark stagnation soaking her whole body also gained momentum from that voice.

"—"

It was no one else. The one she could least face was him. She had wounded him with words she should never have said, left her sins unattended with no way to atone, and was about to be crushed by an unresolvable guilt.

To such a weak, foolish self, Ferris raised his voice.

"Subaru-kun and 'White Sheep'-chan are fighting too! Fight without giving up! Fight… please! Let me fight too!"

Supported by the voice poured over her, she somehow barely resisted the raging blade struck at her. While resisting, a voiceless voice caught in the back of her throat at Ferris's appeal.

Ferris had said he wanted to fight. —The true meaning of that reached her clearly.

And her heart immediately screamed that doing so was impossible.

"It's not impossible. It's not impossible at all. You can do it. —If it's you, Lady Kurush!"

Perhaps reading her weakness from her expression or eyes, it was encouragement that seemed to scoop up her about-to-break heart.

Surely, if it had been anyone but herself, it might have been a rousing cheer that stirred the soul. Yet the helplessness of being unable to meet the expectation placed on her instead robbed her of strength.

The qualification to answer Ferris's direct loyal love belonged only to Kurush Karsten—

"—I've known for a long time! Your memories haven't come back, have they!?"

—In that instant, her breath stopped.

△▼△▼△▼△

She said it. She finally said it.

She knew that saying this would make her show the face she least wanted to see.

"—"

Kurush, her eyes now two colors—amber and gold—wide open, stared at her in shock. Suppressing the urge to look away from that gaze, Felix twisted her body.

Not to regain freedom. But to project her voice from her belly.

"I knew! How long do you think I've been with you!? I've seen through all of it from the start!"

It was a lie. —She had noticed because of these two months.

At first, she had been unable to think properly, because the sight of Kurush grieving from the heart at Felix's decision was so painful. It was a doubt she could hold because there had been time to calm down, and because there had been two months of acting together with the "Six-Tongued."

"But I'm a coward and a sneaky weakling, so I was scared and couldn't confirm it."

Surely that too was one of the countless sins Felix had committed.

"—"

While listening to Felix's appeal, Kurush struck away the shears.

She could not hear any reply from her. Or rather, for some reason, the sounds of the battle between Kurush and the shears man did not reach Felix's ears at all. Silence.

But she could tell her own voice was reaching by Kurush's face, which grew cloudier the more she spoke.

She didn't want to hurt her. She had never thought of driving her into a corner. —But if that drastic treatment was necessary, then even sticking a finger into the wound could be rationalized as treatment.

Because otherwise, she could not save the life of the great fool who had bet on believing in Felix like this, the great fool with no medicine to give and no magic to cast, whose life was about to spill away.

"You're saying you can't, you can't, your head is in complete chaos, right? You're thinking it's impossible for you, that everything before your eyes is pitch black. —But I'll correct one thing in your thinking, Kurush Karsten."

That was the phrasing Kurush had liked to use when exchanging words with others.

Deliberately saying it, Felix stared at the wavering amber and gold—

"—You can do it. If it's you and me, we can do it."

Ah, what a foolish emotional argument, what an unreasonable fatalism.

No, no, she really hated it. She didn't want to look directly at it. She didn't want to admit it. She couldn't accept it.

This was exactly the same as what a certain great fool was doing. —No different from the great fool who believed and hoped on her own, and used that trust as a shield to threaten.

But it couldn't be helped. There was no other move.

Because that very great fool was standing on the edge of a cliff where every second counted—she had no choice but to adopt the great fool's method that had lit a fire in Felix's heart.

"—Lady Kurush!"

At Felix's appeal, at her gaze, Kurush's expression distorted greatly.

Her lips moved, spinning words that made no sound, trying to say it. —That she couldn't.

"—You can!"

She couldn't hear it. So it hadn't been said. She pushed through and pierced with such sophistry.

Grief, anger, longing—she would deal with all of it later. Before life, everything was trivial.

She couldn't? What was the reason? —Because she wasn't the Kurush Karsten she once was?

"You're not the you from back then! I'm asking the Kurush-sama who is here, now!"

"—"

"Let's save it! You and me! This kingdom that His Highness loved!!"

At the voice, the two-colored eyes looked straight at Felix. —In those pupils, a fire was lit.

The white blade was pointed to the heavens, and in the silent world, the whirling wind became a blade and took form.

That was the synonym of the "War Maiden" Kurush Karsten—the flash of salvation that once mowed down the swarm of "Great Rabbits" that attacked her domain, changed their course, and saved many of her people.

—The Hundred-Man Single Slash, with all her might, was unleashed beneath Kugelschreiber Castle.

△▼△▼△▼△

—Until the very last moment of swinging the sword through, hesitation continued to trail behind.

"—"

Her breath made no sound, her voice made no sound, and the sword flash and its result also made no sound.

Still, she had witnessed the result of the released blade. That was at least her duty, having forgotten Kurush Karsten yet tried to impersonate the "War Maiden."

—The dark stagnation that tormented and cursed her was still writhing within her chest now.

It still made Kurush's hands and feet heavy. The negative emotions would grow without limit, and surely by now, they should have covered all of Kurush.

However—

"You bastards! You and that guy, none of ya are sane!"

Reborn's voice echoed through the space where footsteps, sword flashes, and all environmental sounds were kept from being heard.

While cutting away the shears in his hand, she felt keenly that she had done something for which there were no words in return. —Would the Kurush Karsten of old have done this?

She didn't know. And now, that had no meaning.

The one who had been expected by Ferris, believed in, and responded was this Kurush Karsten.

And that self-recognition became a torrent of positive emotion, filling Kurush's mind and body with vitality enough not to lose to the dark stagnation in her chest, and making her resist Reborn as well.

And so, while Kurush drew the enemy's attention—

"Really, you're such a great fool, Subaru-kun, maybe you should just die once?"

"Hey hey, don't say that even as a joke. I don't even want to think about dying."

Beyond Reborn, with whom she crossed steel, she could hear the voices of the two trading insults.

The boy who had barely been scooped up from a near-death state, and the "Blue" who had scooped up his life, were leaning close to each other on the pool of blood as if embracing.

Subaru, with one knee raised, was held from behind by Ferris. —But that it was not an embrace of affection was obvious at a glance from their reluctant faces.

In fact, it had to be that way. —Ferris's healing magic required touching the other's body to activate.

"Rather, you're the one being far more reckless here."

"Same to you."

So saying, Ferris, with a knife growing from his side, his arms cut off by Kurush at the elbows and the parts beyond left in the wall, snorted.

—It was the quarrel of two great fools, who could rightly be called insane.
